{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":72994622,"ns":0,"title":"Supriyo v. Union of India","revisions":[{"revid":1377291884,"parentid":1374851059,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-09-28T19:02:58Z","size":333413,"sha1":"1411cecb79eac2f6547c52638d51385522cd52a9","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Ongoing Indian LGBT rights case law}}\n{{Very long|date=October 2023}}\n{{Infobox court case\n| name = Supriyo v. Union of India\n| judges = {{Abbr|CJI|Chief Justice of India}}  [[D. Y. Chandrachud|D.Y. Chandrachud]], {{Abbr|J.|Justice}} [[Sanjay Kishan Kaul|S.K. Kaul]], {{Abbr|J.|Justice}} [[Shripathi Ravindra Bhat|Ravindra Bhat]], {{Abbr|J.|Justice}} [[Hima Kohli]] {{Abbr|&|and}} {{Abbr|J.|Justice}} [[P. S. Narasimha|P.S. Narasimha]]\n| keywords = [[Right to family life|Right to Family Life]], [[Same-sex marriage|Marriage Equality]], [[Same-sex adoption|Adoption Rights]], [[LGBT parenting|Parenthood]]\n| opinions = The right to marry is a statutory right, not a constitutional right. Therefore, only Parliament can recognize the marriage between non-heterosexual couples. Transgender individuals in heterosexual relationships can marry under existing marriage laws, including all personal (religious) laws.\n| decision by = {{Abbr|J.|Justice}} [[Shripathi Ravindra Bhat|Ravindra Bhat]],  {{Abbr|J.|Justice}} [[Hima Kohli]] {{Abbr|&|and}} {{Abbr|J.|Justice}} [[P. S. Narasimha|P.S. Narasimha]]\n| dissenting = {{Abbr|CJI|Chief Justice of India}}  [[D. Y. Chandrachud|D.Y. Chandrachud]] (in part), {{Abbr|J.|Justice}} [[Sanjay Kishan Kaul|S.K. Kaul]] (in part)\n| number of judges = 5\n| court = [[Supreme Court of India]]\n| citations = {{URL|1=|2={{Abbr|W.P.(C) No.|Writ Petition (Civil) Number}} 1011/2022}} <br />\n{{URL|1=|2={{Abbr|Diary No.|Diary Number}} 36593/2022}}\n[https://api.sci.gov.in/supremecourt/2022/36593/36593_2022_1_1501_47792_Judgement_17-Oct-2023.pdf Copy of the judgment (2023 INSC 920)]\n| date decided = 17 October 2023 <ref>{{Cite web |url=https://api.sci.gov.in/pdf/ListingNotice/16102023_153745.pdf |title=Special Bench comprising Hon'ble The Chief Justice... |date=October 16, 2023 |publisher=Supreme Court of India |access-date=August 27, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231018150034/https://main.sci.gov.in/pdf/ListingNotice/16102023_153745.pdf |archive-date=October 18, 2023}}</ref>\n| full name = Supriyo {{Abbr|a.k.a.|also known as}} Supriya Chakraborty {{Abbr|&|and}} Abhay Dang {{abbr|v.|versus}} Union of India {{abbr|thr.|through}} Its Secretary, Ministry of Law and Justice {{Abbr|&|and}} other connected cases.\n| prior_actions = The [[Supreme Court of India|Court]] transferred to [[Supreme Court of India|itself]] nine ongoing cases from [[High courts of India|High Courts]]: one from [[Kerala High Court|Kerala]] and eight from [[Delhi High Court|Delhi]].<ref name=\":0\" />\n| start date = 25 November 2022<ref name=\":17\" />\n}}'''Supriyo {{Abbr|a.k.a.|also known as}} Supriya Chakraborty {{Abbr|&|and}} Abhay Dang {{Abbr|v.|versus}} Union of India {{Abbr|thr.|through}} Its Secretary, Ministry of Law and Justice {{Abbr|&|and}} other connected cases,''' [[Neutral citation|2023 INSC 920]] are a collection of landmark cases of the [[Supreme Court of India]], which were filed to consider whether to extend [[Right to family life|right to marry and establish a family]] to [[LGBT|sexual and gender minority]] individuals in India.<ref name=\":11\" /> A five-judge [[Constitution bench (India)|Constitution Bench]], consisting of Chief Justice of India [[D. Y. Chandrachud|D.Y. Chandrachud]], Justice [[Sanjay Kishan Kaul|S.K. Kaul]], Justice [[Shripathi Ravindra Bhat|S.R Bhat]], Justice [[Hima Kohli]] and Justice [[P. S. Narasimha|P.S. Narasimha]], heard 20 connected cases brought by 52 petitioners.<ref name=\"sci130323\">Supreme Court Order: {{Harvnb|W.P.(C) No. 1011/2022 (13 Mar 2023)}}</ref>\n\nThe petitioners, couples and individuals from [[LGBT|sexual and gender minority]] communities, request recognition of the right to marry and establish a family based on protections from discrimination, the right to equality, dignity, personal liberty, privacy, and personal autonomy, and freedom of conscience and expression.<ref name=\"allp\" /> [[Delhi Commission for Protection of Child Rights]], a statutory body of the [[Aam Aadmi Party]]-led [[Government of Delhi|Delhi Government]], intervened to support extending the right to marry and adopt for sexual and gender minority individuals.<ref name=\":42\">{{Cite web |last=Sharma |first=Padmakshi |date=6 April 2023 |title=DCPCR Supports Marriage Equality Petitions In Supreme Court; Says Govt Should Create Awareness That Same Sex Marriages Are Normal |url=https://www.livelaw.in/top-stories/dcpcr-supports-marriage-equality-petitions-in-supreme-court-says-govt-should-create-awareness-that-same-sex-marriages-are-normal-225709 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230406161520/https://www.livelaw.in/top-stories/dcpcr-supports-marriage-equality-petitions-in-supreme-court-says-govt-should-create-awareness-that-same-sex-marriages-are-normal-225709 |archive-date=6 April 2023 |website=www.livelaw.in |language=en}}</ref>\n\nThe respondent, the [[Government of India|Union Government]] under the [[Bharatiya Janata Party]] leadership and its statutory body [[National Commission for Protection of Child Rights]], opposes extending the right to marry and establish a family to sexual and gender minority individuals in India, due to societal, cultural and religious history, consistent legislative policy, popular morality and majoritarian views.<ref name=\":18\">{{Cite news |last=Mahajan |first=Shruti |date=12 March 2023 |title=India Government Opposes Same-Sex Marriage in Landmark Hearing |work=[[Bloomberg News]] |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-03-12/india-s-government-opposes-same-sex-marriage-in-landmark-hearing |archive-date=2023-03-12 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230312135837/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-03-12/india-s-government-opposes-same-sex-marriage-in-landmark-hearing |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=centre20230312>Respondent: {{Harvnb|Union of India (12 March 2023)}}</ref><ref name=\"ncpcr\">Intervenor: {{Harvnb|National Commission for Protection of Child Rights (13 April 2023)}}</ref> The State Governments of [[Government of Assam|Assam]], [[Government of Gujarat|Gujarat]] and [[Government of Madhya Pradesh|Madhya Pradesh]] led by the [[Bharatiya Janata Party]], the [[Government of Rajasthan|State Government of Rajasthan]] led by the [[Indian National Congress]], and the [[Government of Andhra Pradesh|State Government of Andhra Pradesh]] led by the [[YSR Congress Party]], intervened to oppose the right.<ref name=\":40\">{{Cite web |last=Kakkar |first=Shruti |date=17 April 2023 |title=Pleas seeking legal validation of same-sex marriage reflect 'urban elitist' views: Centre to SC |url=https://www.newindianexpress.com/nation/2023/apr/17/pleas-seeking-legal-validation-of-same-sex-marriage-reflect-urban-elitist-views-centre-to-sc-2566755.html |website=The New Indian Express |archive-date=18 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231018150038/https://www.newindianexpress.com/nation/2023/apr/17/pleas-seeking-legal-validation-of-same-sex-marriage-reflect-urban-elitist-views-centre-to-sc-2566755.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=\":46\">{{Cite web |last=ANI |date=10 May 2023 |title=Assam, Andhra and Rajasthan opposed legalising same-sex marriage: Centre tells SC |url=https://theprint.in/india/assam-andhra-and-rajasthan-opposed-legalising-same-sex-marriage-centre-tells-sc/1566438/ |website=The Print |language=en-US |archive-date=10 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230510201049/https://theprint.in/india/assam-andhra-and-rajasthan-opposed-legalising-same-sex-marriage-centre-tells-sc/1566438/ |url-status=live }}</ref>\n\nHindu organizations like Shri Sanatam Dharm Pratinidhi Sabha and Akhil Bhartiya Sant Samiti, Islamic organizations like Jamiat Ulema-e-Hind and Telangana Markazi [[Shia Ulema Council]], the women empowerment organization Bharatiya Stree Shakti, and the educational nonprofit organization Kanchan Foundation, intervened to oppose the right.<ref name=\":10\">{{Cite news |last=Rajagopal |first=Krishnadas |date=17 April 2023 |title=The various petitions around same sex marriage |language=en-IN |work=The Hindu |url=https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/the-various-petitions-around-same-sex-marriage/article66748868.ece |issn=0971-751X |archive-date=3 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230503103856/https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/the-various-petitions-around-same-sex-marriage/article66748868.ece |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=\":60\">{{Cite news |last=Sharma |first=Padmakshi |date=1 April 2023 |title='Assault on family system': Jamiat moves plea in SC opposing same-sex marriage |work=www.livelaw.in |url=https://www.livelaw.in/top-stories/supreme-court-same-sex-marriage-jamiat-ulama-i-hind-marriage-of-opposite-sexes-225337 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230402024200/https://www.livelaw.in/top-stories/supreme-court-same-sex-marriage-jamiat-ulama-i-hind-marriage-of-opposite-sexes-225337 |archive-date=2 April 2023}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-05-11 |title=Rajasthan, Assam and Andhra oppose plea on same-sex marriage |url=https://indianexpress.com/article/india/rajasthan-assam-and-andhra-oppose-plea-on-same-sex-marriage-8602885/ |access-date=2023-10-13 |website=The Indian Express |language=en |archive-date=2023-10-16 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231016020340/https://indianexpress.com/article/india/rajasthan-assam-and-andhra-oppose-plea-on-same-sex-marriage-8602885/ |url-status=live }}</ref>\n\nAs the opponents raised concerns over the well-being of children in same-sex families, independent professional association, the [[Indian Psychiatric Society]], supported marriage and adoption rights for sexual and gender minority individuals based on scientific evidence.<ref name=\":6\" /><ref name=\":2\">{{Cite news |date=10 April 2023 |title=Psychiatrists back LGBTQA marriage & adoption rights |work=The Times of India |url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/psychiatrists-back-lgbtqa-marriage-adoption-rights/articleshow/99363478.cms |issn=0971-8257 |archive-date=10 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230410072010/https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/psychiatrists-back-lgbtqa-marriage-adoption-rights/articleshow/99363478.cms |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=\":3\" />\n\n== Case summary ==\nThe petitioners, consisting of couples and individuals from [[LGBT|sexual and gender minority]] communities, requested the Supreme Court to\n* Recognise the [[marriage]] between any two persons, regardless of [[sexual orientation]] and [[gender identity]], under the following marriage laws, by enforcing the [[Fundamental rights in India|fundamental rights]] guaranteed under Articles [[Article 14 of the Constitution of India|14]], [[Article 15 of the Constitution of India|15]], [[Fundamental rights in India#Right To Freedom|19]], [[Fundamental rights in India#Right To Freedom|21]] and [[Freedom of religion in India|25]] of the [[Constitution of India|Indian Constitution]]:\n** [[Special Marriage Act, 1954|Special Marriage Act of 1954]]<ref name=\":48\">The petitions seeking recognition under the Special Marriage Act of 1954 include: {{Harvnb|Nikesh P.P. v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Dr Kavita Arora v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Udit Sood v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Joydeep Sengupta v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Supriyo v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Parth Phiroze Mehrotra v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Aditi Anand v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Utkarsh Saxena v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Nitin Karan v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Kajal v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Akkai Padmashali v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Rituparna Borah v. Union of India}} and {{Harvnb|Harish Iyer v. Union of India}}.</ref>\n** [[Hindu Marriage Act, 1955|Hindu Marriage Act of 1955]]<ref name=\"hmap\">The petitions seeking recognition under the Hindu Marriage Act of 1955 include: {{Harvnb|Abhijit Iyer Mitra v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Nibedita Dutta v. Union of India}} and {{Harvnb|Sameer Samudra v. Union of India}}.</ref>\n** [[Foreign Marriage Act, 1969|Foreign Marriage Act of 1969]]<ref name=\":43\">The petitions seeking recognition under the Foreign Marriage Act of 1969 include: {{Harvnb|Vaibhav Jai v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Joydeep Sengupta v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Zainab J. Patel v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Sameer Samudra v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Utkarsh Saxena v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Nitin Karan v. Union of India}} and {{Harvnb|Amburi Roy v. Union of India}}.</ref>\n* Declare the notice and objection provisions of the [[Special Marriage Act, 1954|Special Marriage Act]] and [[Foreign Marriage Act, 1969|Foreign Marriage Act]] as [[Void (law)|void]], by enforcing the [[Fundamental rights in India|fundamental rights]] guaranteed under Articles [[Article 14 of the Constitution of India|14]], [[Article 15 of the Constitution of India|15]], [[Fundamental rights in India#Right To Freedom|19]] and [[Fundamental rights in India#Right To Freedom|21]] of the [[Constitution of India|Indian Constitution]].<ref name=\"nop\">The petitions challenging the notice and objection provisions of secular marriage laws—the Special Marriage Act of 1954 and the Foreign Marriage Act of 1969— include: {{Harvnb|Utkarsh Saxena v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Nitin Karan v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Kajal v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Amburi Roy v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Akkai Padmashali v. Union of India}} and {{Harvnb|Rituparna Borah v. Union of India}}.</ref>\n* Declare that a foreign-origin sexual and gender minority spouse of an [[Indian citizen|Indian Citizen]] or [[Overseas Citizenship of India|Overseas Citizen of India]] is entitled to apply for registration as an [[Overseas Citizenship of India|Overseas Citizen of India]] under the [[Citizenship Act, 1955|Citizenship Act of 1955]].<ref name=\"cap\">The petitions seeking eligibility for the foreign-origin queer spouse under the Citizenship Act of 1955 include {{Harvnb|Joydeep Sengupta v. Union of India}} and {{Harvnb|Mellissa Ferrier v. Union of India}}.</ref>\n* Declare that a person can nominate anyone in the place of 'next of kin' under all relevant laws by enforcing the [[Fundamental rights in India|fundamental rights]] guaranteed under Articles [[Article 14 of the Constitution of India|14]], [[Article 15 of the Constitution of India|15]], [[Fundamental rights in India#Right To Freedom|19]] and [[Fundamental rights in India#Right To Freedom|21]] of the [[Constitution of India|Indian Constitution]].<ref name=\"rituparna\">Petitioner: {{Harvnb|Rituparna Borah v. Union of India}}</ref>\nThe respondent, the [[Bharatiya Janata Party]]-led [[Government of India|Union Government]], opposed the request, including the extension of the right to marry and establish a family to sexual and gender minority individuals in India, arguing that\n* Non-recognition of the rights of sexual and gender minority individuals to [[Right to family life|marry and establish a family]] does not violate the [[Fundamental rights in India|fundamental rights]] guaranteed under Articles [[Article 14 of the Constitution of India|14]], [[Article 15 of the Constitution of India|15]], [[Freedom of expression in India|19]], [[Fundamental rights in India#Right To Freedom|21]] and [[Freedom of religion in India|25]] of the [[Constitution of India|Indian Constitution]].<ref name=\"centre20230312\" />\n* Legitimate state interests are constitutionally permissible grounds for limiting the legal recognition of marriage following the [[Directive Principles|Directive Principles of State Policy]] and the [[Fundamental Rights, Directive Principles and Fundamental Duties of India#Fundamental Duties|Fundamental Duties]] of the [[Constitution of India|Indian Constitution]].<ref name=\"centre20230312\" />\n* The [[Parliament of India|Legislature]] reflects the collective wisdom of the nation, and it solely possesses the power to enact a law governing human relationships.<ref name=\"centre20230312\" />\n* As the Indian marriage laws do not recognise same-sex marriages, a foreign-origin sexual or gender minority spouse of an [[Indian citizen|Indian Citizen]] or [[Overseas Citizenship of India|Overseas Citizen of India]] is entitled to apply for registration as an [[Overseas Citizenship of India|Overseas Citizen of India]] under the [[Citizenship Act, 1955|Citizenship Act of 1955]].<ref name=\"centre20230412a\">Respondent: {{Harvnb|Union of India (12 April 2023)}}</ref>\n* The challenge to notice and objection provisions of the [[Special Marriage Act, 1954|Special Marriage Act]] and [[Foreign Marriage Act, 1969|Foreign Marriage Act]] and adoption regulations unrelated to same-sex marriage. Therefore, the Supreme Court should exclude those matters from the current Case.<ref name=\"centre20230412b\">Respondent: {{Harvnb|Union of India on Utkarsh Saxena (12 April 2023)}}</ref>\n\n=== Intervenors ===\n\n==== Government ====\nThe [[National Commission for Protection of Child Rights]], a statutory body of the [[Bharatiya Janata Party]]-led [[Government of India|Union Government]], intervened in the Case opposing the extension of the right to adopt for sexual and gender minority individuals.<ref name=\"ncpcr\" />\n\nThe State Governments of [[Government of Assam|Assam]], [[Government of Gujarat|Gujarat]] and [[Government of Madhya Pradesh|Madhya Pradesh]] led by the [[Bharatiya Janata Party]], the [[Government of Rajasthan|State Government of Rajasthan]] led by the [[Indian National Congress]], and the [[Government of Andhra Pradesh|State Government of Andhra Pradesh]] led by the [[YSR Congress Party]], intervened to oppose the right.<ref name=\":40\" /><ref name=\":46\" /> The [[Delhi Commission for Protection of Child Rights]], a statutory body of the [[Aam Aadmi Party]]-led [[Government of Delhi|Delhi Government]], intervened to support extending the right to marry and adopt for sexual and gender minority individuals. The Commission recommended [[#DCPCR-Guidelines|guidelines]] to ensure the well-being of children in same-sex adoptions.<ref name=\"dcpcri\">Intervenor: {{Harvnb|Delhi Commission for Protection of Child Rights (03 April 2023)}}</ref>\n\n==== Religious organisation ====\nHindu organisations Shri Sanatam Dharm Pratinidhi Sabha and Akhil Bhartiya Sant Samiti and Islamic organisation [[Jamiat Ulema-e-Hind]] and Telangana Markazi Shia Ulema Council intervened in the Case opposing the extension of the right to marry and establish a family to sexual and gender minority individuals in India on socio-legal and religious grounds.<ref name=\":10\" /><ref name=\"juehi\">Intervenor: {{Harvnb|Jamiat Ulema-e-Hind (01 April 2023)}}</ref><ref name=\"tmsuc\">Intervenor: {{Harvnb|Telangana Markazi Shia Ulema Council (10 April 2023)}}</ref>\n\n== Background ==\nA gay couple, Nikesh and Sonu, filed a petition seeking legal recognition of their marriage in the [[Kerala High Court]] on 24 January 2020.<ref name=\"nikeshpp\" /> Kerala High Court Justice [[Anu Sivaraman]] admitted the petition on 27 January 2020.<ref name=\":4\" /> Four sexual and gender minority individuals, Abhijit Iyer Mitra, [[Gopi Shankar Madurai|Gopi Shankar M]], Giti Thadani and G. Oorvas, filed a petition seeking legal recognition of marriage in the [[Delhi High Court]] on 8 September 2020.<ref name=\"abhijit\" /> A two-judge Bench of [[Delhi High Court]], consisting of Chief Justice of Delhi High Court [[Dhirubhai Naranbhai Patel|D.N. Patel]] and Justice Prateek Jalan, admitted the petition on 14 September 2020.<ref name=\":5\" />\n\nA gay couple, Supriya Chakraborty and Abhay Dang, filed a petition seeking legal recognition of their marriage in the Supreme Court of India on 14 November 2022.<ref name=\"supriyo\">Petitioner: {{Harvnb|Supriyo v. Union of India}}</ref> A two-judge Bench of the Supreme Court, consisting of Chief Justice of India [[Dhananjaya Y. Chandrachud|D.Y. Chandrachud]] and Justice [[Hima Kohli]], admitted the petition along with another gay couple, Parth Phiroze Mehrotra and Uday Raj Anand, on 25 November 2022.<ref name=\":17\" />\n\nThe Supreme Court bench consisting of Chief Justice of India [[Dhananjaya Y. Chandrachud|D.Y. Chandrachud]] and Justice [[P. S. Narasimha|P.S. Narasimha]] and Justice [[J.B. Pardiwala]] directed [[High courts of India|high courts]] to transfer nine similar petitions— eight from [[Delhi High Court]] and one from [[Kerala High Court]]— to the Supreme Court to consider alongside the original petitioners.<ref name=\":11\">{{Cite news |date=6 January 2023 |title=Supreme Court transfers to itself all petitions on same-sex marriage |language=en |work=[[The Hindu]] |url=https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/sc-transfers-to-itself-all-pleas-pending-before-different-high-courts-on-recognition-of-same-sex-marriages/article66345499.ece |issn=0971-751X |archive-date=17 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230117194348/https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/sc-transfers-to-itself-all-pleas-pending-before-different-high-courts-on-recognition-of-same-sex-marriages/article66345499.ece |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=\":0\">Supreme Court Order: {{Harvnb|W.P.(C) No. 1011/2022 (6 Jan 2023)}}</ref> On 15 March 2023, the Supreme Court admitted 20 connected petitions filed by 52 sexual and gender minority individuals, including 17  couples from sexual and gender minority communities.<ref name=\"allp\" />\n\nMost of the petitioners wanted recognition of right to marry under secular marriage laws—[[Special Marriage Act, 1954|the Special Marriage Act]] and [[Foreign Marriage Act, 1969|the Foreign Marriage Act]].<ref name=\":49\">The petitions seeking recognition under the secular marriage laws—the Special Marriage Act of 1954 and the Foreign Marriage Act of 1969— include: {{Harvnb|Nikesh P.P. v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Vaibhav Jai v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Dr Kavita Arora v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Udit Sood v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Joydeep Sengupta v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Supriyo v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Parth Phiroze Mehrotra v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Sameer Samudra v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Aditi Anand v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Utkarsh Saxena v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Nitin Karan v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Kajal v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Amburi Roy v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Akkai Padmashali v. Union of India}}, {{Harvnb|Rituparna Borah v. Union of India}} and {{Harvnb|Harish Iyer v. Union of India}}</ref> As various news reports,<ref name=\"newsgrp1\">News reports on unwarranted interferences in marriage due to the notice and objection provision of secular marriage laws:<br />{{Cite news |date=2018-07-24 |title=Ghaziabad Mob Assaults Muslim Man for Registering Marriage With Hindu Woman |work=[[The Wire (India)|The Wire]] |url=https://thewire.in/communalism/love-jihad-ghaziabad-muslim-man-beaten |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180725120728/https://thewire.in/communalism/love-jihad-ghaziabad-muslim-man-beaten |archive-date=2018-07-25}}<br />{{Cite news |last=Kurian |first=Shiba |date=2020-07-20 |title=Kerala interfaith couples harassed by right wing vigilantes using marriage notices |work=[[The News Minute]] |url=https://www.thenewsminute.com/article/kerala-interfaith-couples-harassed-right-wing-vigilantes-using-marriage-notices-129053 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200721101004/https://www.thenewsminute.com/article/kerala-interfaith-couples-harassed-right-wing-vigilantes-using-marriage-notices-129053 |archive-date=2020-07-21}}<br />{{Cite news |last=Sodhi |first=Tanishka |date=2021-07-26 |title=How Hindu vigilantes are exploiting a Special Marriage Act clause to target interfaith couples |work=[[Newslaundry]] |url=https://www.newslaundry.com/2021/07/26/how-hindu-vigilantes-are-exploiting-a-special-marriage-act-clause-to-target-interfaith-couples |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210726073007/https://www.newslaundry.com/2021/07/26/how-hindu-vigilantes-are-exploiting-a-special-marriage-act-clause-to-target-interfaith-couples |archive-date=2021-07-26}}</ref> case studies<ref name=\":59\">{{Citation |title=Facing Reality: A Journey on the Path of Choice |date=2010 |type=Case Study |publication-place=[[Lucknow|Lucknow, India]] |publisher=Association for Advocacy and Legal Initiative}}</ref> and official publications of the [[Law Commission of India|Law Commission]]<ref name=\":57\">{{Citation |last=[[Law Commission of India#Twenty-First Law Commission|Twenty-First Law Commission]] |title=Reform of Family Law |date=31 August 2018 |url=https://archive.pib.gov.in/documents/rlink/2018/aug/p201883101.pdf |type=Consultation Paper |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201217035842/https://archive.pib.gov.in/documents/rlink/2018/aug/p201883101.pdf |publisher=[[Law Commission of India]] |archive-date=17 December 2020}}</ref><ref name=\":12\">{{Cite report |url=https://cdnbbsr.s3waas.gov.in/s3ca0daec69b5adc880fb464895726dbdf/uploads/2022/08/2022081053-3.pdf |title=Prevention of Interference with the freedom of Matrimonial Alliances 2012 (in the name of Honour and Tradition): A suggested legal framework |last=[[Law Commission of India#Nineteenth Law Commission|Nineteenth Law Commission]] |date=August 2012 |publisher=[[Law Commission of India]] |issue=242 |access-date=22 February 2023 |archive-date=7 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230207090438/https://cdnbbsr.s3waas.gov.in/s3ca0daec69b5adc880fb464895726dbdf/uploads/2022/08/2022081053-3.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> documented unwarranted interferences in marriage due to the notice and objection provision of secular marriage laws and recommended its removal, most of the petitioners seeking recognition under secular marriage laws challenged the constitutionality of the notice and objection provisions.<ref name=\"nop\" />\n\nSome of the petitioners are practising [[Hindus]] who believe that [[Hinduism]] does not prohibit marriage between sexual and gender minority individuals. They argued that excluding couples from sexual and gender minority communities from the [[Hindu Marriage Act, 1955|Hindu Marriage Act]] amounted to a violation of their [[Freedom of religion in India|freedom to practice their religion]].<ref name=\"hmap\" />\n\nMany advocates represented the petitioners, while [[Attorney-General for India|Attorney General]] [[R. Venkataramani]] and [[Solicitor General of India|Solicitor General]] [[Tushar Mehta]] represent the respondents. The Supreme Court appointed Advocate [[Arundhati Katju]] and Kanu Agrawal as the Nodal Counsel for the petitioners and respondents, respectively.<ref name=\":0\" />\n\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" style=\"margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; border: none;\"\n|+ Cases connected to Supriyo v. Union of India\n|- class=\"static-row-header\"\n! colspan=\"2\" |First Hearing\n! rowspan=\"2\" |{{Abbr|W.P.(C) No.|Writ Petition (Civil) Number}}\n! rowspan=\"2\" |Petitioners\n! rowspan=\"2\" |Respondents\n|-\n!Court\n!Date\n|- style=\"vertical-align: top;\"\n| rowspan=\"3\" data-sort-value=\"1\" style=\"text-align: center;\"|[[Kerala High Court|High Court of Kerala]]\n| rowspan=\"3\" |{{Date table sorting|27 Jan 2020}}<ref name=\":4\">Kerala High Court Order: {{Harvnb|W.P.(C) No. 2186/2020 (27 Jan 2020)}}</ref>\n| rowspan=\"3\" data-sort-value=\"2020-02186\" |02186/2020\n| rowspan=\"3\" |Nikesh P.P. {{Abbr|&|and}} Sonu M.S.<ref name=\"nikeshpp\">Petitioner: {{Harvnb|Nikesh P.P. v. Union of India}}</ref>\n|[[Ministry of Law and Justice (India)|Ministry of Law and Justice]], [[Government of India|Union of India]]<ref name=\"nikeshpp\" />\n|- style=\"vertical-align: top;\"\n|Department of Law, [[Government of Kerala|State of Kerala]]<ref name=\"nikeshpp\" />\n|- style=\"vertical-align: top;\"\n|Marriage Officer, [[Thrissur district|Thrissur]]<ref name=\"nikeshpp\" />\n|- style=\"vertical-align: top;\"\n| rowspan=\"13\" data-sort-value=\"2\" style=\"text-align: center;\"|[[Delhi High Court|High Court of Delhi]]\n|{{Date table sorting|14 Sep 2020}}<ref name=\":5\">Delhi High Court Order: {{Harvnb|W.P.(C) No. 6371/2020 (14 Sep 2020)}}</ref>\n| data-sort-value=\"2020-06371\" |06371/2020 \n||Abhijit Iyer Mitra, [[Gopi Shankar Madurai|Gopi Shankar M]], Giti Thadani {{Abbr|&|and}} G.Oorvasi<ref name=\"abhijit\">Petitioner: {{Harvnb|Abhijit Iyer Mitra v. Union of India}}</ref>\n|[[Ministry of Law and Justice (India)|Ministry of Law and Justice]], [[Government of India|Union of India]]<ref name=\"abhijit\" />\n|- style=\"vertical-align: top;\"\n| rowspan=\"4\" |{{Date table sorting|08 Oct 2020|addkey=1}}<ref>Delhi High Court Order: {{Harvnb|W.P.(C) No. 7657/2020 (08 Oct 2020)}} & {{Harvnb|W.P.(C) No. 7692/2020 (08 Oct 2020)}}</ref>\n| rowspan=\"2\" data-sort-value=\"2020-07657\" |07657/2020\n| rowspan=\"2\" |Vaibhav Jain {{Abbr|&|and}} Parag Vijay Mehta<ref name=\"vaibhav\">Petitioner: {{Harvnb|Vaibhav Jai v. Union of India}}</ref>\n|[[Ministry of Law and Justice (India)|Ministry of Law and Justice]], [[Government of India|Union of India]]<ref name=\"vaibhav\" />\n|- style=\"vertical-align: top;\"\n|Marriage Officer, [[List of diplomatic missions of India|Consulate General]], [[New York City|New York]]<ref name=\"vaibhav\" />\n|- style=\"vertical-align: top;\"\n| rowspan=\"2\" data-sort-value=\"2020-07692\" |07692/2020\n| rowspan=\"2\" data-sort-value=\"Kavita Arora and Ankita Khanna Versus Union of India\" |Dr Kavita Arora {{Abbr|&|and}} Ankita Khanna<ref name=\"kavita\">Petitioner: {{Harvnb|Dr Kavita Arora v. Union of India}}</ref>\n|[[Ministry of Law and Justice (India)|Ministry of Law and Justice]